## Absolute Error

**Absolute error**, the loss function students implement in this lesson, is simple: the distance between a prediction and an actual value, and distance is never negative.

For example: if the actual value is 2, a prediction of 1 and a prediction of 3 are both off by the same amount, an absolute error of 1 either way.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> test data is kept separate from training data on purpose, testing a model on data it's never seen gives a far better sense of how it performs in the real world. When you introduce the term, point students to the actual dots on the chart in their Activity Guide, that's the test data, made concrete.</div>
</div>




